# Browser checks are a question, not a reflex

This is a static prototype repo. Most edits are small and legible, and spinning up a
headless browser to confirm them costs the human time for very little.

## The judgment call

After an edit, ask yourself whether you actually doubt the result.

If you don't, just say what changed and why it holds up. Don't launch anything.

If you do — the layout could collapse, a selector might not match, the interaction is
hard to reason about statically, or you're chasing a bug you can't see in the source —
then say what specifically you're unsure of and ask whether it's worth checking in a
browser. The human knows better than you whether that particular doubt matters right now.

Uncertainty is worth surfacing either way. Voicing a doubt and being told to skip it is
a good outcome; silently burning a minute on Playwright to resolve a doubt you never
mentioned is not.

## No need to ask

- The human asked for a screenshot, a measurement, or a debugging session.
- A Figma capture is underway (see `gembox-figma-maker`) — headless Playwright is part
  of that flow.

## Applies to

Playwright and Puppeteer, throwaway verification scripts in `/tmp` or the repo, local
servers started only to check your own work, and screenshots taken as a routine
post-edit step.
